**One thing worth your attention that I found while checking the ID-safety of that deletion:**

Artifact IDs allocate from git tags only (`max(tag) + 1`, `pkg/scaffold/idresolver.go:73`), with a silent filesystem fallback whenever a git op fails — including `FetchTags` and tag push. This repo has **no remote**, so every recent scaffold has quietly taken the fallback. Result: 88 issue tags (001–088) vs 89 issue files. ISSUE-089 is committed with no reservation tag.

That's harmless today. It stops being harmless the moment you add the real `backstop-ai/backstop-core` remote — a named launch task. `FetchTags` starts succeeding, the tag path reactivates, finds max tag 088, and hands out **089** to the next new issue, colliding with the committed ISSUE-089. The hazard is created by a launch step, so it wants fixing before that step rather than after.

I couldn't file it as an issue (the trigger hook would recurse into me).

